Warrick, Camille,<br />
<br />
I have now read all 24 documents from Marcus's index myself, in date order,=
 and I can add three things to his summary.<br />
<br />
The closing letter of August 9, 2012 does not use the formula we use when w=
e close on corrective action. It does not say "based on the corrective acti=
ons described in your response," because there was no response - there was =
nothing to respond to. It says the staff completed the examination and note=
d no deficiencies. Its second paragraph is the standard reminder that the a=
bsence of deficiencies is not a Commission finding of compliance.<br />
<br />
The two 2013 messages are the ones I would put in front of anyone who still=
 thinks Count II is alive. In February 2013 the registrant's chief complian=
ce officer wrote to the examiner in charge asking whether the firm had any =
outstanding items from the examination "as our new compliance consultant ha=
s asked me for a list." The examiner replied the same afternoon: "You have =
none. 2012-0331 closed clean." That is our own examiner, five months after =
the closing letter, telling the registrant in writing that there is nothing=
 outstanding.<br />
<br />
And the internal sampling messages describe a fee billing test and a custod=
y review that both came back clean, which is presumably where somebody in 2=
013 got the idea that this examination touched custody at all.<br />
<br />
Delphine Karrasch<br />
